mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — pillar sets across business types\n\nReal, ownable pillar sets (illustrative businesses), each contrasted with the **generic version**\nto show the difference. Notice: every pillar is a *theme*, every set has *one signature pillar*\n(⭐), and promotion is the minority. These aren't intents or formats.\n\n---\n\n## 1. B2B SaaS — payroll tool for small-business owners\n- ⭐ **Payroll without a specialist** — the signature POV: this scary, \"expert-only\" task made\n  simple for owners. *(Owns it: that's the whole product thesis.)*\n- **Small-business money mistakes** — the audience's broader pains around finances/taxes.\n- **Behind the build** — transparent product decisions, what they ship and why. *(Connection.)*\n- **Owner wins** — customers who took payroll off their plate. *(Proof / soft conversion.)*\n\n*Generic version (avoid):* \"Educational, product updates, testimonials, behind-the-scenes.\" →\nintents and formats, not themes.\n\n## 2. Ecommerce / DTC — sustainable sneakers\n- ⭐ **Design-first sustainability** — the POV that you shouldn't sacrifice looks to be green.\n- **The materials story** — where the recycled materials come from, how shoes are made.\n- **How to wear them** — styling, outfits, real life. *(Audience-relevant utility.)*\n- **In the wild** — customers wearing them, UGC. *(Proof / community.)*\n\n*Generic version:* \"Product photos, promos, eco facts, reviews.\" → catalog, not pillars.\n\n## 3. Local — neighborhood coffee shop\n- ⭐ **The craft of coffee, demystified** — making good coffee approachable, not intimidating.\n- **Our corner of the neighborhood** — the place, the community, local life.\n- **The people** — baristas, regulars, the owner. *(Connection.)*\n- **What's on this week** — seasonal drinks, events. *(Soft promotion.)*\n\n*Generic version:* \"Menu posts, behind-the-scenes, promotions.\" → no theme to own.\n\n## 4. Professional services — equity-comp financial advisory\n- ⭐ **Equity comp, decoded** — the specialist POV: RSUs/options need a specialist, not a\n  generalist.\n- **Tech-employee money decisions** — the audience's real questions (vesting, taxes, timing).\n- **How specialists think** — frameworks, mental models. *(Credibility.)*\n- **Anonymized client outcomes** — real (compliant) results. *(Proof — within guardrails.)*\n\n*Generic version:* \"Finance tips, market news, company updates.\" → un-ownable and generic.\n\n## 5. Personal brand — indie maker building in public\n- ⭐ **Building in public, real numbers** — radical transparency as the differentiator.\n- **Lessons from failures** — what broke and what it taught. *(Connection + credibility.)*\n- **The craft of {their skill}** — how they actually do the work.\n- **Tools & workflow** — what they use, how. *(Audience-relevant utility.)*\n\n*Generic version:* \"Motivation, tips, my journey, promos.\" → vague and interchangeable.\n\n## 6. Nonprofit — youth literacy\n- ⭐ **Kids choosing their own books** — the signature POV: agency over prescription.\n- **Impact, in numbers and faces** — outcomes + kid stories. *(Serves donors.)*\n- **Why reading changes lives** — the bigger case. *(Awareness.)*\n- **Ways to help** — donate, volunteer, partner. *(The single conversion pillar.)*\n  *(Note: pillars 2 & 4 serve donors/supporters; 1 & 3 serve the broader audience.)*\n\n*Generic version:* \"Stories, stats, donation asks, events.\" → mix of formats and intents.\n\n---\n\n## The pattern across all six\n\n- Every pillar is a **theme the brand can own**, with a real reason.\n- Each set has **exactly one signature pillar** (⭐) carrying the brand's POV.\n- Pillars are about the **audience's world**, not the brand's catalog.\n- Each could generate **dozens of posts** (sustainable).\n- **Promotion is one pillar at most**, the minority.\n- None of them are \"educational / behind-the-scenes / promotional\" — because those aren't\n  pillars.\n\nAim for this level of specificity and ownership every time.\n"
